{% doc %}
  Outputs the WhatsApp number in the form wa.me needs: digits only, including
  the country code, with no +, spaces or punctuation. Outputs nothing at all if
  no number is set.

  wa.me silently fails on a number without a country code — it does not error,
  it just opens a "phone number shared via link is invalid" page. The admin
  field was filled in as a bare 10-digit Indian mobile, so a 10-digit value is
  treated as Indian and given a 91 prefix. Anything 11 digits or longer is
  assumed to already carry its country code and is passed through untouched.

  Capture it, don't print it, when you need the value:

  @example
  {%- capture wa_number -%}{%- render 'whatsapp-number' -%}{%- endcapture -%}
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign wa = settings.whatsapp_number | remove: ' ' | remove: '-' | remove: '(' | remove: ')' | remove: '+' | strip
  if wa == blank
    break
  endif
  assign wa_size = wa | size
  if wa_size == 10
    assign wa = '91' | append: wa
  endif
  echo wa
-%}
